{
  "result": "After rain, the city remembers how to shine. Streetlights tremble in puddles, shop windows glow like small theaters, and every passing bus drags a ribbon of gold across the asphalt.\n\nPeople move differently after weather. They walk more slowly, as if the rinsed air has made room for thoughts usually crowded out. A woman in a yellow coat pauses beneath an awning to watch water spilling from a broken gutter. A child jumps into a puddle with such conviction that strangers laugh, briefly united by the splash.\n\nOn the corner, a baker unlocks her door. Warmth escapes, carrying the smell of cinnamon and yeast. She sets a tray in the window, and soon the street begins choosing its breakfast. Even the stray cat appears, shaking one paw at a time, offended by the sky.\n\nPerhaps this is why I like rain: it interrupts the usual argument between the world and ourselves. It asks nothing grand. It simply falls, darkens the pavement, and gives overlooked things a brightness. A bottle cap becomes a coin. A cracked wall becomes a mural of reflected clouds. A tired face becomes, for one second, part of a moving constellation.\n\nBy evening, the clouds lift. Windows open. Somewhere, someone plays a trumpet badly, bravely, and the notes wander over rooftops. The city does not become beautiful because its problems have disappeared. It becomes beautiful because, for a while, everyone can see the fragile shine, and perhaps remember that ordinary days are still capable of wonder.",
